cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Groundlens in your research, please cite both the software and the paper below."
title: "Groundlens: deterministic first-stage grounding triage for LLM outputs"
abstract: >-
  Groundlens measures whether an LLM answer was drawn from its source using the
  geometry of sentence-embedding space. It computes two deterministic scores —
  the Semantic Grounding Index (SGI), a ratio of angular distances between
  response, question and context, and the Directional Grounding Index (DGI), the
  alignment of the question-to-response displacement with a calibrated reference
  direction — with no LLM in the scoring path. It is designed as the cheap first
  stage of a verification pipeline: it clears the clear cases and escalates the
  rest, and it publishes its own blind spot (in-register factual substitution)
  as a number rather than a footnote.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Marin
    given-names: Javier
    email: javier@jmarin.info
    # TODO(javier): add your ORCID here, e.g.
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
    # An ORCID on the CITATION.cff is what lets indexers tie this software,
    # the arXiv preprints and any future co-authored paper to one identity.
repository-code: "https://github.com/groundlens-dev/groundlens"
url: "https://groundlens.dev"
repository-artifact: "https://pypi.org/project/groundlens/"
license: Apache-2.0
version: 2026.8.5
date-released: "2026-08-05"
keywords:
  - hallucination-detection
  - llm-evaluation
  - embedding-geometry
  - grounding
  - retrieval-augmented-generation
  - ai-safety
  - model-risk-management
